# ADR-0001: Smart-card authentication for OpenShift

- **Status:** Proposed
- **Date:** {{ .Date }}
- **Generated by:** auth-accelerator (`{{ .Pattern.ID }}`)

## Context
A cluster requires {{ .Env.SmartCard }} smart-card authentication for operators and
users. Available components: OpenShift **{{ .Env.OCPVersion }}** ({{ .Env.OCPFlavor }}),
identity provider **{{ .Env.IDP }}**, **{{ .Env.PKI }}** PKI. Group source is
**{{ .Env.GroupSource }}**.{{ if .Env.FIPS }} The cluster runs in **FIPS** mode.{{ end }}{{ if .Env.AirGapped }} The environment is **air-gapped**.{{ end }}

## Decision
Use **{{ .Pattern.Title }}**. The IdP performs x509 smart-card validation and issues
OIDC tokens; OpenShift consumes them directly via the `Authentication` CR
(`type: OIDC`, integration `{{ .Pattern.OCPIntegration }}`), available from
OCP {{ .Pattern.MinOCPVersion }}+. The built-in OAuth server is retired.

## Consequences
- Single cluster-wide OIDC provider; multiple IdPs are not supported on this path.
- A break-glass kubeconfig is mandatory operational state, not optional.
- Certificate→user mapping must be maintained at the IdP ({{ .Env.GroupSource }} source).

## Alternatives considered
- Built-in OAuth server with an OIDC identity provider (retained pre-4.20; more limited).
- Request-header / SAML proxy (only when the IdP cannot speak OIDC, e.g. legacy ADFS).
